Physical Constraints: The "Forbidden Transitions" Problem
After a big storm, the ground is soaked. Tomorrow's humidity cannot be 0%. This isn't just a probability of 0.001 — it's a thermodynamic impossibility.
Why? After 30mm of rain, soil moisture is at field capacity. Evaporation over 24h even in full sun can only return ~2-5mm of water vapor to the air column. With saturated soil, the minimum next-day humidity is ~60-70%, not 30%, not 0%.
This means the transition matrix should be sparse — not just low probabilities, but structural zeros:
FROM state = "storm" (high wind, precip > 10mm, humidity > 90%):
  TO "dry_sunny" (humidity < 30%)    → PHYSICALLY FORBIDDEN (structural zero)
  TO "partly_cloudy" (humidity 40-60%) → EXTREMELY UNLIKELY (near-zero)
  TO "overcast_drizzle" (humidity 70-85%) → PLAUSIBLE (system recovering)
  TO "rain_continuing" (humidity 85-95%)  → PLAUSIBLE (system persisting)
  TO "cold_front_clearing" (temp drops, wind shifts, humidity 60-75%) → PLAUSIBLE
These constraints dramatically reduce the degrees of freedom. Instead of learning 100 transition probabilities (10×10 states), we learn ~30 structurally possible ones.
