Deep Hidden Markov Model (3 Layers)
The visible weather is the bottom layer. Above it are unobserved drivers:
LAYER 3 (Planetary / synoptic, 1000-3000km, weeks):
  States identified by meteorologists on pressure charts:
    Ω₁: "Azores High extension over central Europe"
    Ω₂: "Scandinavian blocking high"  
    Ω₃: "Icelandic Low train (zonal flow)"
    Ω₄: "Cut-off low over Mediterranean"
    Ω₅: "Omega block (high-low-high)"
  
  These persist 5-21 days and determine the "weather type."
  Under Ω₁: warm, dry summer or cold, dry winter (depending on season).
  Under Ω₃: train of rain systems every 2-3 days (classic European autumn).
LAYER 2 (Regional / mesoscale, 100-500km, 1-5 days):
  States:
    H₁: "anticyclonic_dome" (subsiding air, clear skies, temperature inversion)
    H₂: "pre_frontal" (approaching system, falling pressure, increasing cloud)
    H₃: "frontal_passage" (wind shift, precipitation band, rapid change)
    H₄: "post_frontal" (cold advection, cumulus streets, showery)
    H₅: "convective_building" (surface heating, instability building)
    H₆: "convective_release" (thunderstorm outbreak, rapid pressure changes)
    H₇: "marine_layer" (coastal stratus, onshore flow, diurnal burn-off)
    H₈: "radiation_fog_regime" (clear nights, near-zero wind, inversion)
LAYER 1 (Local observable, single city, daily):
  The 20D weather vector emitted from the Layer 2 hidden state.
The key property: Layer 2 states are inferred, not observed. You see the 20D vector but you don't know whether today's rain is from a cold front (→ clearing tomorrow) or from orographic uplift against mountains (→ rain continues). The HMM learns to distinguish these from the sequence of observations, because they have different transition patterns.
